// loft#801 — one error inside a module must not invent a SECOND one saying a type
// is undefined when the importer declares it.
//
// `parse_file` used to return on the first Error BEFORE draining `todo_files`, the
// list of files SUSPENDED at a `use`.  Here that list holds `fwd`, which is waiting
// for `inner` and which declares `Colour`.  Abandoning it meant `Colour` never
// registered, so the run added `Undefined type Colour` — pointing away from the one
// mistake the author made, at a line where the declaration is plainly visible.
//
// What this baseline pins is the ABSENCE of that second error — and it now pins it
// the strongest way available: the whole chain COMPILES AND RUNS, so there is no
// error to invent a second one from.  It captured one error until loft#803 landed
// (a forward-referenced enum VALUE did not resolve, and said so as `Unknown type
// null — did you mean 'JNull'?', naming two things nobody wrote).  If `todo_files`
// is ever abandoned again, `Colour` stops registering and this exits non-zero.
use fwd::*;
fn main() {
  s = mk();
  println("{s.s_a}");
}
